WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.477
Dev and test splits: iterate on one, report the other.

2
00:00:03.757 --> 00:00:10.712
You will revise the judge prompt several times: sharpen the criterion, add an edge case, swap a few-shot example.

3
00:00:10.992 --> 00:00:19.632
Every revision made while looking at a set of examples fits the prompt to those examples, so agreement measured on the same set is optimistic.

4
00:00:19.912 --> 00:00:24.435
It’s the same overfitting you’d guard against in any model, applied to a prompt.

5
00:00:24.715 --> 00:00:26.400
The fix is the standard one.

6
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:37.923
Hamel’s guide suggests three splits of the labeled data: Train (roughly 10 to 20 percent): clear pass and fail examples you may paste into the prompt as few-shot examples.

7
00:00:38.203 --> 00:00:44.496
Dev (roughly 40 to 45 percent): run the judge, read every disagreement, revise the prompt.

8
00:00:44.776 --> 00:00:47.187
Dev examples never go into the prompt.

9
00:00:47.467 --> 00:00:54.123
Test (roughly 40 to 45 percent): untouched until you freeze the prompt; then run once and report.

10
00:00:54.403 --> 00:00:56.493
Two implementation details matter.

11
00:00:56.773 --> 00:01:05.093
Stratify by label, so both splits keep the same pass and fail mix; otherwise TNR on a test set with three failures is noise.

12
00:01:05.373 --> 00:01:19.197
And make the split deterministic: assign each example by hashing its id with a fixed seed, not by an unseeded shuffle, so the split is the same on every machine and nobody can reshuffle until the numbers look better.

13
00:01:19.477 --> 00:01:20.715
The lab does both.

14
00:01:20.995 --> 00:01:22.104
How many labels?

15
00:01:22.384 --> 00:01:35.291
Hamel suggests aiming for 30 to 50 examples of each label in dev and in test where you can, and warns that with fewer than about 60 examples confidence intervals are often too wide to be useful.

16
00:01:35.571 --> 00:01:37.107
With a TNR of 0.

17
00:01:37.387 --> 00:01:42.912
875 measured on only 8 failures, one standard error is about 0.

18
00:01:43.192 --> 00:01:43.960
12.

19
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.803
If your calibration set is small, say so next to every number.
